2. STEP 1 – IDENTIFY CERTIFICATE HOSTNAMES
===============================================================================

Before raising the CMP request, identify every hostname that is expected to use
the certificate.

For CRM-to-Olympus, the primary application hostname is:

olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

The application also uses the following VIPs:

PROD:
vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

COB:
vs-bula-ifev.nam.nsroot.net


COMMON NAME – CN

The Common Name represents the primary identity of the certificate.

For CRM-to-Olympus:

CN = olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net


SUBJECT ALTERNATIVE NAMES – SAN

SAN means Subject Alternative Name.

SAN entries allow additional DNS names to be covered by the same certificate.

For our certificate the required DNS names are:

DNS:
olympus.api.crmtoolympusnew.cloudgsl.nam.nsroot.net

DNS:
vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

DNS:
vs-bula-ifev.nam.nsroot.net


WHY SAN ENTRIES ARE IMPORTANT

When a client connects to:

https://vs-hdax-dxbg.nam.nsroot.net

the certificate must be valid for that hostname.

If the hostname being accessed is not present in the certificate CN/SAN,
hostname validation can fail even when the certificate itself is otherwise
valid.

Therefore, before submitting CMP, confirm all DNS names that legitimately need
to use the certificate.

Do not add unrelated application servers or random hostnames.


