8. STEP 7 – OPTIONAL CERTIFICATE COMPARISON
===============================================================================

If required during troubleshooting, compare the certificate provided by CMP
with the certificate inside the JKS.


CHECK CMP CERTIFICATE:

keytool -printcert -v -file Certificate.cer.txt


OR CHECK SHA-256 FINGERPRINT:

openssl x509 -in Certificate.cer.txt -noout -fingerprint -sha256


CHECK JKS:

keytool -list -v -keystore cacerts.jks


Compare the SHA-256 fingerprint.

The certificate fingerprint should match.


If the separate certificate and private-key files need to be validated against
each other, compare their derived public keys.


CERTIFICATE:

openssl x509 -in Certificate.cer.txt -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey -pubin -outform pem | openssl sha256


PRIVATE KEY:

openssl pkey -in PrivateKey.key.txt -pubout -outform pem | openssl sha256


The two hashes should match.

If they do not match, the private key does not belong to that certificate.


