14. OUR CERTIFICATE JOURNEY / LESSONS LEARNED
===============================================================================

The following section captures the approaches we used during implementation and
why the final approach changed.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14.1 INITIAL APPROACH – DOWNLOADED EXISTING JKS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Initially, we downloaded an existing JKS referenced through the available
Confluence/PKI documentation.

We then generated/added application key material using keytool and used the JKS
for the application.

The initial approach was approximately:

Existing downloaded JKS
        |
        v
Generate/add self-signed application certificate/key pair
        |
        v
Upload JKS
        |
        v
Deploy application


A representative keytool operation for generating a self-signed key pair looks
similar to:

keytool -genkeypair -alias <alias> -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -keystore <jks-file> -validity <days> -dname "CN=<hostname>"


NOTE:

This is included only to document the historical approach.

It is NOT the current certificate-creation process for CRM-to-Olympus.


One of the earlier certificates also showed an incorrect application identity
such as:

CN = Pratap Ganji

and the certificate validity was reaching:

January 06, 2026


This was not appropriate as the long-term application server identity.


