Email indicating that the initial approach was not Citi standard.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14.3 CORRECTION – RAISED CMP CERTIFICATE REQUEST
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We then raised the approved CMP request.

CMP generated:

Certificate.cer.txt

PrivateKey.key.txt

CertAndKey.pfx


This resolved the certificate-generation issue because the application
certificate and private key now originated from the approved CMP process.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14.4 SECOND APPROACH – CMP CERTIFICATE IMPORTED INTO EXISTING CONFLUENCE JKS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

After receiving the CMP certificate, the next approach was:

CMP
 |
 v
CertAndKey.pfx
 |
 v
Import certificate/key into existing Confluence JKS
 |
 v
Application JKS


This was better than the first approach because the certificate itself originated
from CMP.

However, we were still depending on the previously provided base JKS.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14.5 ISSUE – CONFLUENCE/MICROSOFT-PROVIDED JKS TO BE DECOMMISSIONED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Later, we were informed that the JKS being referenced through the existing
Confluence process was Microsoft-provided/legacy trust material and that this
approach was being decommissioned.

Therefore, we should not continue building our application's certificate
solution on top of that JKS.


Key learning:

Do not introduce a dependency on an unrelated or legacy JKS when the CMP PFX
already contains the application's certificate and matching private key.


