15. DO'S
===============================================================================

DO:

1. Raise the application certificate through Citi CMP.

2. Confirm the CN before submitting CMP.

3. Identify all required SAN entries before submitting CMP.

4. Include only legitimate application/VIP DNS names.

5. Download the CMP-generated PFX securely.

6. Validate the PFX before creating the JKS.

7. Confirm the PFX contains:

   PrivateKeyEntry

8. Verify:

   CN
   SAN
   Issuer
   Validity
   serverAuth
   Key size

9. Convert the PFX directly into JKS.

10. Validate the generated JKS.

11. Confirm PrivateKeyEntry still exists in the JKS.

12. Check the certificate expiration date.

13. Deploy the JKS through the standard application deployment process.

14. Verify the deployed file timestamp.

15. Validate the certificate presented by the live HTTPS endpoint.

16. Run an actual functional POST request.

17. Review application logs after deployment.

18. Start certificate renewal well before expiration.

Current expiration:

August 04, 2028


===============================================================================
16. DON'TS
===============================================================================

DON'T:

1. Do not create the PROD application identity using a self-signed certificate
   unless explicitly approved for that use.

2. Do not generate a random/private key outside the approved process for the
   production certificate.

3. Do not download a generic JKS and assume it is appropriate for this
   application.

4. Do not reuse a Microsoft/legacy JKS simply because it exists in older
   documentation.

5. Do not import only the certificate and forget the private key.

6. Do not proceed if JKS shows only trustedCertEntry when a server
   PrivateKeyEntry is required.

7. Do not ignore SAN entries.

8. Do not ignore certificate expiration.

9. Do not expose private keys in Confluence.

10. Do not expose PFX/JKS passwords.

11. Do not commit passwords or private-key text into source control.

12. Do not assume every HTTP error is an SSL issue.

HTTP 400:
Usually request/payload/header related.

HTTP 404:
Usually URL/path/routing related.

HTTP 405:
Usually wrong HTTP method.

SSLHandshakeException / PKIX / hostname verification:
Investigate certificate/TLS.


